---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleFeedback from "../../components/ArticleFeedback.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="When Your Dating Photos Don't Look Like You Anymore"
    description="A dating profile can create a very different expectation when your photos are heavily edited, outdated, or simply don't look much like you anymore."
>
    <main class="article-page">
        <article>
            <header class="article-header">
                <p class="eyebrow">DATING PROFILE PHOTOS</p>

                <h1>When Your Dating Photos Don't Look Like You Anymore</h1>

                <p class="article-intro">
                    A dating profile can look great and still create the wrong
                    first impression.
                </p>
            </header>

            <div class="article-content">
                <p>
                    Imagine you have been talking to someone on a dating app for
                    a few days. Their photos look great. You have a pretty good
                    idea of what they look like, what they usually wear, even
                    what kind of places they like.
                </p>

                <p>Then you meet them in person.</p>

                <p>
                    And there is that strange little moment when your brain
                    quietly says, “Oh.”
                </p>

                <p>
                    Nothing is necessarily wrong. They are still the same
                    person. But something doesn't match.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Maybe their photos were heavily edited. Maybe every picture
                    was taken from the most flattering angle. Maybe the photos
                    are five years old. Or maybe they simply look very different
                    in normal life than they do in the tiny collection of
                    carefully selected images on the profile.
                </p>

                <p>That gap matters more than people sometimes realize.</p>

                <h2>Your photos are setting an expectation</h2>

                <p>
                    A dating profile is not just showing someone what you look
                    like. It is quietly telling them what they can expect to see
                    if they meet you.
                </p>

                <p>
                    That doesn't mean every photo needs to be completely
                    unedited or brutally honest. Nobody is asking for a passport
                    photo taken under fluorescent lights in a DMV.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The problem starts when the difference between the photo and
                    the person becomes large enough that the other person has to
                    mentally adjust when they meet you.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A little difference is normal. Everyone looks slightly
                    different depending on the light, camera, expression,
                    hairstyle and what they happen to be wearing that day.
                </p>

                <p>
                    But if your profile consistently shows the most polished,
                    most edited and most carefully controlled version of you,
                    that version becomes the person the other person expects to
                    meet.
                </p>

                <p>And expectations are surprisingly powerful.</p>

                <h2>The problem isn't looking good</h2>

                <p>
                    There is nothing wrong with wanting your dating photos to
                    look good.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Of course you should choose flattering photos. Good light
                    helps. A good camera helps. Wearing something you feel
                    comfortable in helps. Even a little editing can make a photo
                    look more like the moment actually felt.
                </p>

                <p>The problem is not improvement.</p>

                <p>It is replacement.</p>

                <p>
                    There is a difference between removing a temporary blemish
                    and changing the structure of your face. There is a
                    difference between correcting the color and making your skin
                    look like it belongs to someone who has never encountered a
                    bad night's sleep.
                </p>

                <p>One makes the photograph look better.</p>

                <p>The other starts creating a different person.</p>

                <h2>
                    People notice the mismatch even when they can't explain it
                </h2>

                <p>This is one of the interesting things about photos.</p>

                <p>
                    Someone looking at your profile usually isn't analyzing your
                    jawline, skin texture or camera angle. They are forming a
                    general impression.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Does this person look familiar? Comfortable? Like someone I
                    could actually meet for coffee?
                </p>

                <p>
                    If the photos are heavily edited, extremely posed or all
                    taken under very controlled conditions, the viewer may build
                    an image of you that is more polished than realistic.
                </p>

                <p>Then the real-world version has to compete with it.</p>

                <p>That is a terrible job for a first date.</p>

                <p>
                    You don't want the other person spending the first ten
                    minutes comparing reality with your profile.
                </p>

                <p>You want them thinking about the conversation.</p>

                <h2>
                    The strangest part is that you may not notice it yourself
                </h2>

                <p>
                    There is another problem with editing your own photos: after
                    looking at the same picture enough times, it starts to feel
                    normal.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You see the edited version, then another edited version,
                    then another. Eventually that becomes the version of
                    yourself you are used to seeing on your screen.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Meanwhile, everyone else is seeing you without all that
                    context.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This is one reason getting another person's opinion can be
                    surprisingly useful.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Not because your friend knows which photo will get the most
                    likes. That is a different question.
                </p>

                <p>Ask them something much simpler:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>“Which of these actually looks like me?”</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>
                    That question can produce a very different answer from
                    “Which one makes me look best?”
                </p>

                <h2>
                    Your best dating photo may not be your most impressive one
                </h2>

                <p>
                    This is where the idea of a dating profile gets interesting.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The goal isn't to build the most impressive collection of
                    photographs possible. You are not applying for a job as a
                    professional model.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You are giving another person enough information to imagine
                    meeting you.
                </p>

                <p>
                    That means a photo where you look relaxed, recognizable and
                    genuinely engaged with something can sometimes do more work
                    than a technically perfect portrait.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A slightly messy café. A normal haircut. A photo where your
                    smile isn't perfectly symmetrical. The expression you make
                    when someone says something funny.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Those details can make the person in the photograph feel
                    easier to imagine in real life.
                </p>

                <p>And that is a useful kind of attractiveness.</p>

                <h2>Think about the first meeting, not just the first swipe</h2>

                <p>
                    A dating profile has one very important job: get the right
                    person interested enough to meet you.
                </p>

                <p>That changes how you should think about your photos.</p>

                <p>
                    Instead of asking only, “Does this make me look good?” try
                    asking, “Would someone recognize me from this photo?”
                </p>

                <p>
                    If the answer is yes, you're probably giving them something
                    useful.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You can still look your best. You can choose good lighting,
                    flattering angles and photos you genuinely like. You can
                    remove distractions and make small corrections.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Just leave enough of the real person in the photograph that
                    there isn't a completely different person waiting at the
                    coffee shop.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Because the best outcome isn't for someone to think, “Wow,
                    their photos were amazing.”
                </p>

                <p>It's for them to think,</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        “Yep. That's exactly who I thought I was going to meet.”
                    </p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>
                    That kind of match may be less dramatic than a perfect
                    profile photo.
                </p>

                <p>
                    But it gives you something much more useful: a first
                    impression that survives contact with real life.
                </p>
            </div>

            <ArticleFeedback
                article="when-your-dating-photos-dont-look-like-you-anymore"
            />
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .article-page {
        width: min(100% - 48px, var(--container));
        margin: 0 auto;
    }

    .article-header {
        max-width: 820px;
        padding: 120px 0 100px;
    }

    .article-header h1 {
        margin: 0;
        max-width: 820px;
        font-size: clamp(48px, 6vw, 76px);
        line-height: 1;
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    }

    .article-intro {
        max-width: 600px;
        margin: 32px 0 0;
        font-size: 20px;
        line-height: 1.55;
        color: var(--color-muted);
    }

    .article-content {
        max-width: 680px;
        padding: 80px 0 160px;
        border-top: 1px solid rgba(36, 36, 36, 0.12);
    }

    .article-content p {
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
        font-size: 19px;
        line-height: 1.75;
    }

    .article-content h2 {
        max-width: 680px;
        margin: 5rem 0 1.5rem;
        font-size: clamp(30px, 4vw, 42px);
        line-height: 1.08;
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    }

    .article-content blockquote {
        margin: 2rem 0;
        padding-left: 1.5rem;
        border-left: 2px solid rgba(36, 36, 36, 0.18);
    }

    .article-content blockquote p {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 21px;
        line-height: 1.6;
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
        .article-page {
            width: min(100% - 32px, var(--container));
        }

        .article-header {
            padding: 72px 0 80px;
        }

        .article-header h1 {
            font-size: clamp(42px, 11vw, 58px);
        }

        .article-intro {
            font-size: 18px;
        }

        .article-content {
            padding: 56px 0 100px;
        }

        .article-content p {
            font-size: 17px;
            line-height: 1.7;
        }

        .article-content h2 {
            margin-top: 4rem;
            font-size: 32px;
        }

        .article-content blockquote p {
            font-size: 19px;
        }
    }
</style>
